    "abstract": "We investigate the relationship between recursive observer traces and emergent spectral\n     geometry in hierarchical Markov systems. Building on observer-first approaches and recent\n     discussions of recursive trace logic, we interpret observer windows as coarse-grained dynamical\n     traces over hidden-state Markov processes. We then construct induced Laplacian geometries\n     from these traces and study their spectral properties using diffusion-based spectral dimension\n     measures.\n         The central result is that observer traces generate effective geometries distinct from ordinary\n     visible-state subgraphs. In particular, hidden-state excursions of the form B(I − C)−1 D alter\n     diffusion structure and effective spectral dimension. A sequence of four toy models is presented:\n     a baseline grid trace, a hierarchical trace system, a robustness/control experiment, and a direct\n     trace-versus-subgraph comparison. The final comparison shows that hidden excursions are not\n     equivalent to simple node deletion; they induce effective long-range connectivity in the observer’s\n     visible world.\n         These results do not derive spacetime, gravity, or quantum theory. They provide a toy-model\n     framework in which observer-relative geometry can be studied as an emergent consequence of\n     hidden dynamical organization.",
